WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.220
Imagine waking up on the beach before dawn.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.320
The glimmer of light leads you to the shoreline.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.400
Suddenly, a wave pulls you in

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.490
at first you use the direction of the waves to guide you.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.520
But once you're in the open sea with powerful currents

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.020
bombarding you and very little light—

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.810
How can you be sure of where you are

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.350
and where you're going?

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:25.160
For a hatchling sea turtle

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:27.960
the answer is magnetoreception.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.740
That's the ability to sense magnetic fields.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:32.640
We know many animals use this

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.070
sixth sense for navigation.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.440
What we don't know is exactly

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.230
how magnetoreception works.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.060
Here's what we do know:

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.780
Earth itself is like a giant magnet.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.720
The motion from its liquid outer core

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.120
generates a magnetic field.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:54.080
Certain animals can sense this field

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:57.000
and use it as a compass to tell them

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.340
if they're heading in the right direction

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.220
and a map to give them sign posts

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:02.960
along the way.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:04.880
There's two competing theories

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:07.380
for how magnetoreception works.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.439
One is a chemical sensor/ the other is

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:11.480
a mechanical sensor.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.020
The first theory is that animals have tiny magnetite

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:17.700
particles in their bodies that act as

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:19.280
magnetic receptors.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.060
Magnetite is the most

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.100
magnetic natural metal on earth.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:24.780
It's been found in many animals

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:26.240
that exhibit magnetoreception.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:27.479
And it's thought that

00:01:27.479 --> 00:01:29.640
it's the only potential sensor that

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.170
would be sensitive enough to capture

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:34.650
these incredibly tiny variations in

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:37.079
magnetic field strength that would allow

00:01:37.079 --> 00:01:39.499
the animal to not just know

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.240
whether they're going north or south along the

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:42.600
magnetic field line but know the

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.149
precise beach that they need to get to.

00:01:45.149 --> 00:01:47.909
The other theory is that animals possess

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:50.220
a protein in their eyes called

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.680
cryptochrome which allows them to see

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.540
magnetic fields.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.840
Cryptochrome has been found in the eyes

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.940
of several migratory birds.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:02.340
But we haven't proven either theory for a few reasons.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.940
With magnetoreception you don't know where to look.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:07.160
Magnetic fields pass invisibly through

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:10.940
the entire body so researchers don't  know exactly

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:13.000
where these magnetite particles or

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.800
cryptochromes would be attached to particular cells

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:18.160
and so mistakes are made  all the time.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:21.310
And so far, cryptochrome experiments have only

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:23.799
yielded positive results in the presence

00:02:23.799 --> 00:02:26.359
of magnetic fields much stronger than Earth's.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.440
The frontier is now not so much at the animal behavior

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:33.060
level but actually getting inside the brains of

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:36.400
these animals and trying to find these

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.159
sensing cells and connecting them to the

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:40.619
neural circuitry.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.420
It's not so much a question of which animals

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.500
have this sense but which don't.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.800
So, researchers have said: "Why not humans?"

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.420
Maybe we had this sense at one point deep in our

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:53.889
evolutionary past and and lost it.

00:02:53.889 --> 00:02:55.669
But maybe there's a vestige left.

00:02:55.669 --> 00:03:00.879
Researchers in California and Japan have gone after

00:03:00.879 --> 00:03:03.670
this holy grail one more time.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:06.879
With a very specialized experiment.

00:03:06.879 --> 00:03:09.670
One that relies on double blinding and

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:12.760
magnetic shielding and they're seeing

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:15.699
glimmers maybe even more than glimmers

00:03:15.699 --> 00:03:19.419
of this magnetic sense in humans.

00:03:19.419 --> 00:03:21.099
It's starting to be reproducible

00:03:21.099 --> 00:03:22.599
and they're really excited about it.

